$sql = @'
-- ===== Ture and the man's fish =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Ture and the man's fish$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Among the many recorded adventures of the Zande trickster Ture, one of the best known appears in Mrs Edward Clive Gore's 1921 collection <em>Sangba Ture</em>: the tale of Ture and a man's fish.</p><p>A hardworking Zande man spent hours baling water from a forest pool to catch a basket of small, sweet fish. As he rested beside his catch, Ture crept through the bushes, eyeing the fish with envy. He wanted them badly, but had no wish to do the work of catching them himself.</p><p>So he turned to <em>sanza</em>, the Zande art of speech with a hidden, double meaning. Approaching the fisherman in apparent panic, Ture cried: "My friend! Look at the sky — the termites at your mounds are swarming at this very moment! Leave your fish with me, and run to gather your harvest!"</p><p>Terrified of losing his termites, the fisherman thanked Ture and ran into the forest. The moment his back was turned, Ture grabbed the basket and made off with it.</p><p>At the termite mounds, the man found nothing swarming. Realising he had been tricked, he rushed back to find his basket empty and Ture long gone. "It is witchcraft!" he cried, as the Azande do when sudden misfortune strikes.</p><p>To find the culprit, he consulted the termite oracle, open to men, women and children alike. He planted a branch of the <em>dakpa</em> tree and one of the <em>kpoyo</em> tree in a mound and waited overnight for an answer. By morning, the termites had eaten only the <em>dakpa</em> branch — proof, by the oracle's logic, that Ture was the thief.</p><p>Villagers marched to Ture's home and found him bloated and asleep after his stolen feast. The story is told to Zande children as a warning: greed brings swift shame, and the oracles will always uncover those who use sanza to deceive their neighbours.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '0564b691-1b4a-4be4-9f53-32fa5f1f9849';

-- ===== The tale of Ture, Leopard and the rubbing-board =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$The tale of Ture, Leopard and the rubbing-board$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Ture, the trickster spider of Zande folklore, was walking through the forest when he caught the scent of roasting meat. Leopard had just returned from a successful hunt and was preparing a feast — and Ture, greedy as ever, wanted it for himself.</p><p>He tried <em>sanza</em>, the Zande art of speech carrying a hidden meaning, hoping to confuse Leopard and slip away with the food. Leopard was not fooled, and suspected an attempt to steal his meat.</p><p>To protect his feast, Leopard consulted <em>benge</em>, the supreme poison oracle. He mixed the alkaloid paste and administered it to a fowl, asking: "If Ture means to steal my meat, let the fowl die." The fowl convulsed and died — the oracle's verdict was final.</p><p>Ture, meanwhile, wanted to know if his trick would work. He visited his one true friend, the operator of his rubbing-board oracle, and asked whether he would succeed. The wooden handle stuck fast to the board — a bad omen. His friend warned him: go back, and you will be punished.</p><p>Ture ignored the warning. He crept back to steal the meat while Leopard slept, but Leopard was waiting. In the struggle that followed, Leopard threw Ture into the cooking fire, burning his back — the origin, Zande storytellers say, of the tale "How Ture burnt Leopard all over".</p><h2>What the story teaches</h2><p>First published in Mrs Edward Clive Gore's 1921 collection <em>Sangba Ture</em>, the tale carries two lessons Zande audiences would have recognised immediately.</p><p>It is the source of a well-known Zande proverb — that Ture "had only one operator of the rubbing-board oracle" — used to observe that a person has many acquaintances but very few true friends willing to tell them the truth.</p><p>It also shows Ture ignoring sacred guidance out of unchecked greed, teaching children the boundaries of proper conduct through comedy rather than direct instruction.</p>$body$
WHERE id = 'd9191f40-18a3-4b1d-89a0-4ee3045f35ed';

-- ===== Three things that make Zande a remarkable language =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Three things that make Zande a remarkable language$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Beyond their historical beliefs in witchcraft, the Azande are known for a language with features that set it apart from most others in the region.</p><h2>A language spanning three countries</h2><p>The Azande are spread across three countries — the north-east of the Democratic Republic of Congo, western South Sudan and the eastern Central African Republic. Their language, known natively as Pazande, has an estimated 1.8 million first-language speakers, making it the largest of the Zande languages.</p><h2>Counting without separate number words</h2><p>The traditional Zande counting system is base-20, built around counting fingers and toes. Because the language historically lacked standalone words for numbers above ten, larger numbers are expressed descriptively: 20 is <em>boro ru e</em>, literally "a person stands it" — the sum of one person's fingers and toes. Six is <em>bisue bati sa</em>, "five over one", built on the verb meaning "to step over". Thirty becomes "20+10", and 120 is rendered as "one hundred and two-ten".</p><h2>Grammar built on splitting words in two</h2><p>Zande grammar uses structures rarely seen in European languages. Modifiers often split in half to surround the word they describe: the demonstrative "this" (<em>gi...re</em>) wraps around a noun, so "this man" becomes <em>gi ko re</em>. Negation works the same way, enclosing verb phrases with <em>nga...te</em> or <em>nga...ya</em>.</p><p>The language also keeps strict grammatical divisions between humans, animals and inanimate objects, each with its own pronouns and possessive forms. And it chains actions together using a single consecutive marker, <em>ki</em>: <em>Ko a ndu ki mangi e ki yega</em> — literally "he went and did it and came home" — describes a whole sequence of actions in one breath.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '66240dcf-ae5b-4fb6-9a6d-cf0ef79b5b3d';

-- ===== Zande subject, object and possessive pronouns =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Zande subject, object and possessive pronouns$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Zande distinguishes between personal, animal and neuter (inanimate) classes across its subject, object and possessive pronouns.</p><h2>Subject and object pronouns</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Meaning</th><th>Subject</th><th>Object</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I / me</td><td>mi</td><td>re</td></tr><tr><td>You (singular)</td><td>mo</td><td>ro</td></tr><tr><td>He / him</td><td>ko</td><td>ko</td></tr><tr><td>She / her</td><td>ro</td><td>ri</td></tr><tr><td>He/she (indefinite)</td><td>ni</td><td>ni</td></tr><tr><td>We / us</td><td>ani</td><td>rani</td></tr><tr><td>You (plural)</td><td>oni</td><td>roni</td></tr><tr><td>They / them</td><td>i / yo</td><td>yo</td></tr><tr><td>It (animal)</td><td>u</td><td>ru</td></tr><tr><td>They (animal)</td><td>ru</td><td>ra</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Animal object pronouns are also suffixed to nouns to show close possession, such as body parts. A noun ending in <em>-se</em> drops that syllable first: <em>ngbaduse</em> ("chest") becomes <em>ngbadure</em> ("my chest").</p><h2>Possessive pronouns</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Meaning</th><th>Form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mine</td><td>gimi</td></tr><tr><td>Yours (singular)</td><td>gamo</td></tr><tr><td>His</td><td>gako</td></tr><tr><td>Hers</td><td>gari</td></tr><tr><td>His/hers (indefinite)</td><td>gani</td></tr><tr><td>Its (animal)</td><td>gau</td></tr><tr><td>Its (neuter)</td><td>gaa</td></tr><tr><td>Ours</td><td>gaani</td></tr><tr><td>Yours (plural)</td><td>gaoni</td></tr><tr><td>Theirs</td><td>gayo</td></tr><tr><td>Theirs (animal)</td><td>gaami</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Possessive pronouns can also work reflexively, as in <em>Mi ye ti gimi</em> ("I have come myself").</p><h2>Reflexive pronouns</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Singular</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st person</td><td>tire</td><td>tirani</td></tr><tr><td>2nd person</td><td>tiro</td><td>tironi</td></tr><tr><td>3rd masculine</td><td>tiko</td><td>tiyo / tira</td></tr><tr><td>3rd feminine</td><td>tiri</td><td>tiyo / tira</td></tr><tr><td>Animal</td><td>tiru</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Neuter</td><td>tie</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>$body$
WHERE id = '4f2d5602-7306-4d7b-b4a1-bc3a463af920';

-- ===== What role does the akpaningba xylophone play in Zande dance? =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$What role does the akpaningba xylophone play in Zande dance?$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Carved from tree trunks and strung with keys of dried banana stem, the akpaningba is the instrument the Zande people of Central Africa build their dances around. Musicians and researchers describe it as far more than accompaniment — it is the pulse a whole village moves to, and a way of saying, in music, what might otherwise go unsaid.</p><p>Also spelled akpaningbo or kpaningbo, the instrument belongs to a family of African log xylophones found across the Congo basin, where related versions go by dozens of local names. Among the Zande — spread today across South Sudan, DR Congo and the Central African Republic — this design has given its name to an entire genre of circle dance, the kpaningba, built specifically around it.</p><h2>An instrument made to be shared</h2><p>The akpaningba is what organologists call a "loose-key" xylophone: wooden bars laid across a resonating base, sometimes bundles of reeds or banana stems, rather than fixed to a frame. Field recordings from Zande territory show it typically built and played by three or four men at once, each responsible for a different part of the melody. One archival description from a village near Juba calls it simply the beating heart of both the music and the village, since it is usually the only strictly melodic instrument in an ensemble otherwise built from drums, bells and rattles.</p><p>Tuning is exact, not approximate. Recordings catalogued by the International Library of African Music show the akpaningba's bars tuned to a fixed sequence of pitches matching Zande harp strings, meaning the instrument shares a tonal vocabulary with the rest of Zande music rather than standing apart from it. Younger players often learn on scaled-down versions — one documented example, made by a boy from three sections of tree trunk with five keys of banana stem, shows how directly the skill passes down within families.</p><h2>At the centre of the circle</h2><p>In performance, the akpaningba sits at the centre of the dance, not the edge. Dancers move in a circle around the instrument and its players, taking small, unison steps that carry the ring gradually anti-clockwise. A recurring gesture among Zande dancers involves holding the hands out, palms upward, gently raising and lowering the forearms in time with the beat.</p><p>The xylophone does not follow the dancers. The dancers follow the xylophone. From that central position, the instrument effectively conducts the event — its players set and shift the tempo, cue changes in the song, and give singers their starting pitch.</p><h2>More than entertainment</h2><p>Zande communities have long treated music and dance as a channel for collective feeling, alongside storytelling and song. Occasions built around the akpaningba have served as outlets for celebration — weddings, harvests, homecomings — and as a space where a community's mood, including frustration or shared hope, could be voiced through song lyrics carried on the xylophone's rhythm, in a culture where direct complaint was not always the easiest route.</p><p>This echoes something wider in Zande culture: the same community that developed sanza, the art of layered, double-meaning speech, also built a musical form in which a joyful surface can carry a more serious undertone.</p><h2>Made by the people who play it</h2><p>Unlike instruments bought ready-made, the akpaningba is typically built by the same local artisans, often musicians themselves, who go on to perform on it. That link between maker and player reinforces its status as a communal object rather than a specialised piece of equipment — understood locally less as an instrument in the abstract sense and more as a living expression of Zande identity and craftsmanship.</p><h2>A tradition under pressure</h2><p>Decades of displacement in South Sudan, driven by civil conflict, have scattered many Zande communities away from the settled village life in which traditions such as the kpaningba dance were normally passed from elders to children. UNESCO's office in South Sudan has pointed to the country's traditional dances as part of an intangible heritage under strain, and has backed cultural festivals bringing different ethnic groups together to perform and safeguard their dance traditions.</p><p>At a local level, Zande village associations and dance groups continue to organise around instruments like the akpaningba specifically to keep the skills of building and playing it alive, teaching the next generation of musicians rather than letting the tradition lapse.</p><p>For now, the akpaningba remains in active use in the villages where it is still built. Whether at a wedding, a harvest gathering or an evening when musicians decide to play, it continues to do what it has done for generations: pull a community into a circle and give shape, in rhythm rather than words, to whatever the moment calls for.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>World Music Network / Riverboat Records, sleeve notes for Wayo, "Trance Percussion Masters of South Sudan" (2014)</li><li>International Library of African Music (ILAM) field recordings, "Kponingbo xylophone" and "Zande dance", via SAMAP, University of KwaZulu-Natal</li><li>Pitt Rivers Museum, Southern Sudan Project, E. E. Evans-Pritchard photographic collection</li><li>Royal Museum for Central Africa (Africamuseum), "Anemba" instrument entry</li><li>UNESCO, "Celebrating culture, identity and diversity for social cohesion, unity and peace in South Sudan"</li><li>Encyclopaedia Britannica, "South Sudan: Music, Dance, Poetry"</li></ul>$body$
WHERE id = '28fa9fa2-fc7f-445c-b46e-bd7d0cdc90e2';

-- ===== Who is Ture and how do his stories guide behaviour? =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Who is Ture and how do his stories guide behaviour?$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Among the Azande people of Central Africa, one character turns up in almost every traditional tale: a cunning, reckless, endlessly scheming figure named Ture. His misadventures have entertained and instructed generations, and anthropologists say they still offer a window into how a stateless, oral society taught its members right from wrong.</p><p>Described by researchers as the chief character in Zande folktales, Ture is a trickster, a stock figure found in oral literatures across the world, from Anansi the spider in West Africa to Coyote in Native American traditions.</p><p>What sets Ture apart is not heroism but its opposite. He is greedy, vain, impulsive and frequently outwitted by his own cunning. Almost every tale collected from Zande storytellers over the past century features him in some capacity, according to the British anthropologist Sir Edward Evan Evans-Pritchard, who documented Zande life more extensively than any outsider before or since.</p><h2>A society without a word for fiction</h2><p>The Azande are thought to have migrated into the upper Nile basin and the fringes of the Congo rainforest from further west, beginning roughly four centuries ago. For much of their history they lived in scattered homesteads rather than towns, organised around kinship, chiefly authority and a set of beliefs in which witchcraft, known as mangu, explained most misfortune. Storytelling, alongside song and instrumental music, was one of the principal forms of evening entertainment, but it was never only entertainment.</p><p>Ture's escapades are shot through with a distinctly Zande verbal art called <em>sanza</em>, speech or action carrying a deliberate double meaning. Sanza was not confined to folktales: it structured real conversations between princes and commoners, between spouses, at beer parties and in courtship, allowing a speaker to say one thing while meaning another. Ture's stories are, in effect, sanza performed at length, and audiences were expected to catch the second, unspoken meaning beneath his antics.</p><blockquote>Ture could be regarded as a collective manifestation of Azande personality — E. E. Evans-Pritchard</blockquote><h2>Comedy with a warning inside it</h2><p>Trickster tales rarely hold their central character up as an example to follow. Ture is not a role model. He is a cautionary tale with legs.</p><p>Story after story shows him lying, stealing or betraying friends and family. Story after story shows those choices collapsing on him, often painfully.</p><p>Researchers say this pattern served two purposes in a community with no police, courts or written law. First, it deterred bad behaviour: audiences absorbed, through laughter, what recklessness actually cost. Second, it drew the boundaries of acceptable conduct by dramatising what happened to those who crossed them.</p><p>Recorded story titles give a sense of the range: "How Ture killed his father", "How Ture burnt Leopard all over" and "Ture, the youth, the old woman, and the ripe fruit" are among dozens catalogued by Evans-Pritchard and earlier collectors.</p><h2>How the tales were written down</h2><p>For centuries the stories existed only in performance. That changed in the early twentieth century, largely through missionary contact.</p><p>The most significant early collection, <em>Sangba Ture</em>, was assembled by Mrs Edward Clive Gore, whose husband served with the Church Mission Society in Yambio, now in South Sudan. It was first published in London in 1921.</p><p>Many of the tales were first transcribed by a Zande scholar at a nearby Catholic mission, according to Evans-Pritchard. The manuscript passed to a British colonial district commissioner, Major Larken, before reaching Gore for publication. A Zande contributor, Enoka Mangbondo, wrote the foreword and is thought to have supplied further stories.</p><p>Evans-Pritchard built on this record during fieldwork in the 1920s and 1930s. He published his own translations in academic journals and, in 1967, in a dedicated volume, <em>The Zande Trickster</em> — still the most complete scholarly record of Ture's adventures in English.</p><h2>The oracle and the storyteller</h2><p>Zande social life revolved heavily around consulting oracles to identify the source of misfortune. A simple rubbing-board oracle handled minor problems. The more solemn poison oracle, known as benge, was reserved for serious matters and traditionally the preserve of men.</p><p>These ritualised occasions became gathering points where stories, proverbs and songs were rehearsed and kept alive.</p><p>Proverbs kept Ture in circulation too. One recorded saying — that Ture had only one operator of the rubbing-board oracle — was used to observe that a person truly has very few real friends among many acquaintances.</p><h2>A tradition under pressure</h2><p>Colonial administrations, citing public-health concerns such as the control of sleeping sickness, moved many Azande out of scattered homesteads and into linear villages along straight roads, often alongside strangers rather than relatives. Mission schools introduced literacy but, in many cases, treated indigenous storytelling as something to be replaced rather than preserved.</p><p>Later conflict in South Sudan pushed large numbers of Azande into DR Congo, Uganda and the Central African Republic, further straining how oral literature is passed down.</p><p>Despite this, researchers say the Ture stories have endured because they carry a community's shared values in a portable, memorable form — one that survives displacement better than written records alone.</p><h2>Rethinking Ture today</h2><p>Ture has not stayed confined to academic journals. His tales have been retold in anthologies of African literature and mythology, reaching readers with no connection to the Nile-Congo divide.</p><p>Some recent scholarship has begun questioning the colonial-era record itself. Researchers argue that Evans-Pritchard and other early collectors cast Ture too neatly as a culture hero, underplaying the creative agency of the Zande storytellers who shaped each telling. Others have re-examined the female characters who appear opposite Ture, questioning readings of the tales as straightforwardly patriarchal.</p><p>What is not seriously disputed is Ture's centrality to Zande self-understanding. By making foolishness, greed and betrayal look ridiculous rather than admirable, his stories quietly tell a community who it wants — and does not want — its members to be.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E. E. Evans-Pritchard, "Four Zande Tales", Journal of the Royal Anthropological Institute, 1965</li><li>E. E. Evans-Pritchard, "Some Zande Folk-Tales from the Gore Collection", Anthropological Quarterly, 1966</li><li>E. E. Evans-Pritchard, "Sixty-One Zande Proverbs", Man, 1963</li><li>E. E. Evans-Pritchard (ed.), The Zande Trickster, Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1967</li><li>Wikipedia, "Ture (Zande character)"</li><li>Countries and Their Cultures / Encyclopedia.com, "Azande"</li></ul>$body$
WHERE id = 'e32c7bec-f943-452f-b156-a02879082ebb';

-- ===== Staying connected to home across generations =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Staying connected to home across generations$title$,
  body = $body$<p>For Azande families who have settled outside South Sudan, DR Congo and the Central African Republic, in Europe, North America, Australia, the Middle East and beyond, staying connected to language, culture and extended family often becomes harder with each generation.</p><p>Children raised abroad may grow up speaking the language of their new country fluently, while Zande becomes something spoken only with grandparents, or not at all. Traditions once passed down naturally in daily life can start to feel distant or unfamiliar.</p><p>This is not a problem unique to the Azande; it is something every diaspora community navigates. But it is worth actively working against, rather than accepting as inevitable.</p><h2>What this platform offers</h2><p>This site is one small tool for that effort: a place to read real history, contribute to a living dictionary, and stay connected to community news from home — wherever home now means.</p><p>If you are raising children in the diaspora, or navigating this yourself, we would welcome your reflections as a post here. Your experience is exactly the kind of story this space was built to hold.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '43a1a459-3236-477a-aa29-a76751fbbbbd';

-- ===== Why community organisations matter to this platform =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Why community organisations matter to this platform$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Long before this website existed, Azande community life was already organised through churches, schools, youth associations, elders' councils, hometown unions and diaspora associations spread across three countries and beyond.</p><p>This platform does not aim to replace any of that work. It exists to make it more visible and more connected.</p><h2>How to take part</h2><p>If your organisation is active — a church group in Yambio, a diaspora association in Europe or North America, a youth group, or an informal support network — this is a place to introduce that work to the wider Azande community.</p><p>A good community post describes what your group does, who it serves, and how others can get involved. Real organisations doing real work, made visible to people who might never have heard of them otherwise.</p><p>If you lead or are part of a community group, consider writing a short introduction. Community grows stronger when people know what already exists.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '12f913e5-61d1-4e10-afcd-adcf4114fd25';

-- ===== What makes Pa Zande a distinctive language =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$What makes Pa Zande a distinctive language$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Zande (Pa-Zande) belongs to the Ubangian branch of the Niger-Congo language family — a group spoken across Central Africa, distinct from the Bantu languages more commonly known outside the region.</p><p>Its closest relatives are Nzakara, Barambu and Geme, together forming the small Zande language cluster. Outside this group, Zande has few close linguistic relatives, making it a fairly distinct branch within Central African languages.</p><h2>A tonal language</h2><p>Like many African languages, Zande makes meaningful use of tone: the pitch at which a word is spoken can change its meaning entirely, even when the letters are identical. This is one reason Zande is genuinely difficult to learn from text alone — hearing it spoken matters.</p><p>Estimates of the number of Zande speakers vary by source, generally ranging from roughly one million to several million people, spread across South Sudan, DR Congo and the Central African Republic.</p><p>If you speak Zande, consider contributing to our community dictionary. Every word added helps keep the language documented and accessible for the next generation.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '0ee2b688-5bf8-42be-b43a-f684cf9bdc15';

-- ===== Naming traditions among the Azande =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Naming traditions among the Azande$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Among the Azande, a person's name is rarely just a label. It often carries meaning, history or belief.</p><p>Many traditional Zande names are theophoric, referencing Mbori, the Zande concept of a Supreme Being. Names such as Rokombori, Sangbaambori and Moabimbori call on the divine in daily identity.</p><h2>Two naming traditions, side by side</h2><p>With the spread of Christianity across Azande communities, naming traditions evolved further. Many names today blend both worlds — Zande tradition and Christian faith — such as Mbikoyesu, Mboriundo and Mborifuhe. It remains common for a person to carry both a Zande name and a Christian name.</p><p>Naming conventions also differed by social class historically. The anthropologist E. E. Evans-Pritchard documented that certain names among the Avongara royal line were reserved for kings and rarely, if ever, given to commoners. A name itself could signal status and lineage.</p><p>If your family carries naming traditions, meanings or stories worth preserving, we would welcome you sharing them — either as a post here or as a note on our Heritage page.</p>$body$
WHERE id = 'd67e35f6-bcc2-4410-884f-d9338ffec68c';

-- ===== How colonial partition divided Azande territory =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$How colonial partition divided Azande territory$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Before the early 1900s, Azande territory was not divided by the international borders that separate it today. The Azande kingdoms, though politically independent from one another, formed a connected cultural and political world stretching across what are now three countries.</p><p>That changed after King Gbudwe's death in 1905. In the years that followed, the territory was formally split among three competing colonial powers: Britain took the portion that became part of Sudan, and later South Sudan; Belgium absorbed Azande land into the Congo Free State and later the Belgian Congo; and France claimed the portion that became French Equatorial Africa, and eventually the Central African Republic.</p><p>The partition was decided by colonial powers, not by the Azande themselves, and it cut across existing kingdoms, clans and family ties. It is the direct reason Azande communities today live under three different national governments, speaking different colonial languages — English and French — alongside Zande, despite sharing one people, one history and one language at their core.</p><p>The modern borders that separate Azande families were drawn without them. The shared identity that predates those borders is part of why platforms like this one exist.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '6a36af7a-cd24-480a-b270-f88047d2315a';

-- ===== How to share local news from your area =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$How to share local news from your area$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Local news works best when it comes directly from people living the story, not from a distant newsroom. If something is happening in your town, village or neighbourhood that other Azande people would want to know about, you are the right person to write about it.</p><p>A good local news post is short, clear and answers the basics: what happened, where and when. You do not need to be a professional journalist — just honest and accurate.</p><p>Some ideas: a new school or clinic opening, a road or bridge being repaired, a local market or harvest update, weather affecting your area, or any development your community should know about.</p><p>Log in, click "Write a post", choose Local News, and share what you know. Every contribution makes this space more useful for everyone.</p>$body$
WHERE id = 'b6621077-0068-4de1-9264-e19540607e39';

-- ===== New safety and trust features now live on Azande News =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$New safety and trust features now live on Azande News$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Since launching, we have added several features to keep Azande News safe, trustworthy and welcoming for everyone in the community.</p><p>Every new post is now checked by an automated system for harmful content — hate speech, harassment, threats or spam — before it reaches readers. New contributors' first few posts also get a quick review to help build trust as the community grows.</p><p>We have added two-factor authentication for account security, the option to permanently delete your account and personal data at any time, and basic protections against spam and abuse.</p><p>You can also bookmark articles to read later, message other members directly (with mutual consent before any conversation starts), and contribute words to our growing Zande dictionary.</p><p>Thank you for being part of building this platform. If you notice anything that does not feel right, use the Report button on any post, or reach out through our Contact page.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '133a206a-ac3e-4691-95b6-1ef596be8c13';

-- ===== A space for the Azande diaspora =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$A space for the Azande diaspora$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Azande people live not only across the Democratic Republic of Congo, South Sudan and the Central African Republic, but around the world — in Europe, North America, Australia, the Middle East and beyond.</p><p>This section is for that diaspora: stories about Azande life abroad, associations and gatherings in diaspora communities, reflections on staying connected to home from a distance, and news relevant to Azande people living outside the three home countries.</p><p>If you are part of the diaspora, we especially want to hear from you. This platform exists to connect Azande people everywhere, and that only works if the diaspora is genuinely part of it, not an afterthought.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '10f95f99-4c0c-4330-9e15-24c732425fdb';

-- ===== What belongs in Community =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$What belongs in Community$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Community is for posts about the people, groups and organisations that make up Azande life — associations, churches, schools, youth groups, elders' councils, and the everyday work of people building and supporting community, wherever they are.</p><p>If you are part of an organisation or group doing work worth sharing, or want to introduce your community to others on this platform, this is the place.</p><p>Over time, we hope this section reflects the real organisations and efforts already active across Azande communities — let this be a place to make that work visible.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '46b87685-9d3c-4eba-beca-e9b753584a25';

-- ===== About the Zande language, and our new dictionary =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$About the Zande language, and our new dictionary$title$,
  body = $body$<p>The Azande speak Zande (also written Pa-Zande), part of the Ubangian branch of the Niger-Congo language family. It is the largest of the Zande languages, which also include closely related languages such as Nzakara, Barambu and Geme.</p><p>Estimates of Zande speakers vary by source, ranging from around one million to several million people across South Sudan, DR Congo and the Central African Republic.</p><p>We have launched a community-built Zande dictionary on the site, where anyone can contribute a word and its English translation. Every submission is reviewed before it goes live, to keep the dictionary accurate and reliable.</p><p>If you speak Zande, please consider contributing — even a handful of words helps build something lasting for the next generation. Visit the Zande dictionary under Language to get started.</p>$body$
WHERE id = 'b50a203f-4cfd-4f0c-aa1c-ffa9c7343fad';

-- ===== The traditional way of life among the Azande =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$The traditional way of life among the Azande$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Traditionally, the Azande lived in scattered family homesteads rather than dense villages. Chiefdoms were governed by Avongara nobility, who appointed sons or trusted deputies to administer local territory.</p><p>Farming has always been central to Azande life. Staple crops include cassava, maize, sorghum, millet, groundnuts and sesame, alongside fruits such as mangoes, oranges and bananas. Cassava and peanuts also served as cash crops, with hunting historically supplementing agriculture.</p><p>Marriage traditionally involved a bride wealth payment — historically around twenty spears from the groom's family to the bride's. Zande clans are patrilineal, and among commoners, marriage occurs outside one's own clan.</p><p>Religion today among the Azande is predominantly Christian, introduced during the colonial and missionary era, though elements of traditional Zande spiritual belief — including reverence for Mbori, the Zande concept of a Supreme Being, and ancestor veneration — continue to shape culture and naming traditions.</p><p>We would love this space to grow with real stories and photos from Azande communities today. If you have something to share about daily life, food, celebrations or tradition, please write a post.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '876db836-13a6-468c-99f4-ed8b84e8c3b9';

-- ===== King Gbudwe and the last days of Azande independence =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$King Gbudwe and the last days of Azande independence$title$,
  body = $body$<p>The most thoroughly documented Azande ruler in history is King Gbudwe Bazingbi, who ruled from roughly 1870 until 1905, based near what is now Yambio, South Sudan.</p><p>Gbudwe led sustained resistance against slave raiders, Mahdist forces, and eventually the colonial expansion of Britain and Belgium into Azande territory. In 1904 he led an assault on Belgian-built forts, which was repelled with heavy losses, weakening his position as allied vassals began defecting to colonial powers.</p><p>In February 1905, a British military patrol reached his settlement. Oral histories recorded by the anthropologist E. E. Evans-Pritchard describe Gbudwe being found wounded and captured; he died within days.</p><p>His death marked the end of independent Azande rule. Azande territory was subsequently partitioned among three colonial powers — British, Belgian and French — establishing the borders that still separate Azande communities across South Sudan, DR Congo and the Central African Republic today.</p><p>The Avongara royal line did not end there. In February 2022, Gbudwe's great-grandson, Atoroba Peni Rikito, was formally installed as King of the Azande Kingdom, restoring the monarchy after 117 years.</p><p>Read more on our Heritage page. If your family holds additional verified history, we welcome your corrections and contributions.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '0fc2c8ff-aa72-426b-892d-932ade33ef9a';

-- ===== What belongs in Local News =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$What belongs in Local News$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Local News is where community members share everyday happenings relevant to Azande communities in Western Equatoria, DR Congo, the Central African Republic and beyond — things that do not fit neatly into Culture, History, Language, Community or Diaspora.</p><p>If something is happening in your area that other Azande people would want to know about — local developments, education, health, agriculture, or anything of shared interest — this is the place to write about it.</p><p>As this platform grows, we hope Local News becomes a real, trusted source of everyday news for the community, written by the community itself.</p>$body$
WHERE id = 'dccdd03e-24a6-4370-b3d7-3e212f14e1a9';

-- ===== Welcome to Azande News =====
UPDATE public.posts SET
  title = $title$Welcome to Azande News$title$,
  body = $body$<p>Azande News is now live — a shared space for the Azande people of the Democratic Republic of Congo, South Sudan and the Central African Republic, and the diaspora around the world, to read and share news, culture, history and language.</p><p>This is a community-run project, built by and for the Azande people, wherever they call home. Whether you trace your family to Yambio, Isiro, Rafaï, or anywhere else the Azande have lived, worked and built community, this platform is for you.</p><p>Here you can read news relevant to Azande communities, learn about Azande history, culture and the Zande language, contribute to a growing Zande dictionary, write and share your own posts, and connect with other members through comments.</p><p>This is just the beginning. The more the community contributes, the richer this space becomes. Thank you for being part of it from the start.</p>$body$
WHERE id = '44e1edbf-25bf-42d6-8615-e0fc0c55c28e';

